    "summary": " Insecure Preservation of SUID/SGID Bits during ZIP Extraction Leading to Potential Privilege Escalation",
    "details": "The `zip` crate preserves privileged Unix permission bits (SUID, SGID, and sticky bits) from ZIP `external_attributes` during extraction, instead of masking them to safe permission bits. If a privileged process (for example, `root` in an installer, CI/CD pipeline, or container build) extracts an attacker-controlled ZIP archive using `ZipArchive::extract()` or the stream extraction API, files can be created as setuid-root or setgid. This behavior allows the creation of SUID/SGID files during extraction, which can lead to local privilege escalation depending on the execution environment.\n\nFor further details see: https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/security/advisories/GHSA-vv5m-jxxh-4627",
    "severity": [
      {
        "type": "CVSS_V3",
        "score": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H"
      }
    ],
